er  lippincott schwartz's lab  and sarah cohen was nice enough to give  us  the rights to show this image where  you're seeing different organelles and  their distribution within  just one cell and so what they did was  they used a tool  that's in a previous version of a mars  called distance transformations  to help them understand how far away  objects are from one another so how far  away  are in this case these are mitochondria  and we have other types of organelles in  the different colors here  how far away or how often are each of  these organelle types touching another  organelle type  and as i said in the past we had this  through  a tool called distance transformation  and now we have that built into amaris  with the newest version  9.5 and then over here on the right hand  side  instead of using our surface tool like  sarah did instead this lab was able  to segment out their their mitochondrial  label that they have you can see the  fluorescent image on the left-hand side  and they used the filament model and so  this model is  able to give you um measurements for the  length  of the mitochondria even if they are  curvy like a snake  that sort of thing and so you can see  that model here it's less of a blobby  model  and more of a a column or a rope  something like that to do that analysis  and characterization and so  in this example they were looking over  time and they could see how  the shape of the mitochondria changed  over time  and both of these are found within our  learning center  at omarus.com and you can look up these  names to find out more  about exactly what they did and then  find the original paper  as well so today i hope you've learned a  little bit about how a mars could help  you with your own research  if you are interested in learning more  just let us know we'll make sure to  reach out to you  you can also go on to our website to  find more as well  and now i think claudia is gonna get  started  and um so i'm gonna send it over to her  thanks  thank you meredith and thank you dr  chakravarti for your presentations and i  will wrap  up presenting the dragonfly multi-mode  system  and showing why it is an essential  system an outstanding system for imaging  intracellular dynamic events  so common challenges in the imaging  interstellar dynamic events  include avoid phototoxicity and  photobleaching  and while avoid phototoxicity and  photobleaching researchers  also need to acquire the images with  enough temporal  resolution to visualize the dynamic of  the event that they are  acquiring and studying such as either  being  a very fast dynamic event or  a very long time time-lapse experiment  also the accurate spatial resolution  is needed to capture all the information  of the biological process  and sometimes lifestyle super resolution  it is also required  the throughput of data to increase the  productivity researchers  will benefit on being able to image  multiple imaging fields  in a single experiment to have more data  to analyze and have their  experiments to being statistically  significant  allow the access of multiple imaging  modalities  will allow the researcher to tackle  different biological problems  or use different imaging technologies  to answer one biological question  for fluorescence illumination microscopy  we have  white field imaging where all the light  that goes through the sample is captured  by the detector  turf imaging where the light the sample  is imaged  in an angle and only a scene that  section  at the surface of the sample is captured  by the detector  and the confocal techniques point  scanner and confocal where a single  pinhole discards the auto focus light  and spinning disk multiple pinholes  will discard the autofocus light and in  the next slide i will compare  point scannings with spinning disk  dragonfly  so single point scanning confocal will  scan the image point by point by point  and this as you can imagine as you can  see it is a very long lasting process  whereas the dragonfly multi-point  confocal will deliver  the image in an instant  furthermore dragonfly benefits from  endor's  emc cds and nc and scmos cameras  technology  with up to 90 percent of comp quantum  efficiency  capturing all the photons nearly all the  photons  that come from your imaging whereas the  single point scanning confocals the  pmt's photomultiplier tubes have  up to at the most 45 percent of quantum  efficiency  dragonfly multi-point scanning confocal  is based on the dual micro length  spinning technology  it is a high contrast multi-modal  photoform  it is an instant confocal because it  delivers confocal image in an instant  but it is a multimodal platform because  besides  of being a cofocal it also delivers  laser white field imaging super  resolution techniques such as this storm  and surf stream and turf imaging  dragonfly comes with the borealis  illumination system  allowing a very flat uniform  unilamination  across the whole wavelength from the  visible  to the near infrared wavelengths  key specifications of the dragonfly  include up to 400 frames per second in  the confocal mode  a very large field of view and the very  broad  excitation wavelength and highly  uniformity  across the excitation wavelength with a  16-bit  dynamic range of capturing the image and  this is very important because you can  acquire  all the signals in an image from the  very dim  to the very high signals without having  imaging saturation  or losing any of the low signals  therefore it is not surprising that a  dragonfly  can be used for a very broad range of  applications  such as live and fixed cell imaging  membrane dynamics  cell and developmental biology cancer  research  organoid biology neuroscience and single  molecule  localization imaging  so i will now present real life examples  of  overcoming life imaging challenges with  the drug  this example researchers were able to do  a time lapse for 48 hours  using the fishy system acquiring images  with a double color simultaneously and  even 48 hours of  imaging the cell cycle dynamics cells  were still  dividing happily due to the  dragonfly spinning disk system  but if you want to acquire very fast  events such as  calcium imaging you can also use the  dragonfly acquiring images for example  at 53 frames per second and with the  dragonfly you can acquire  dynamic events up to 400 frames per  second  but sometimes researchers need super  resolution  and need super resolution in live cell  the problem is that  techniques that deliver super resolution  most of the times are not compatible  with life cell imaging  and why due to very complex sample  preparation  due to very long times required to  deliver an image  and very importantly due to the high  energy requirements  to deliver a super resolved image in  2016  professor ricardo hicks have developed  surf  which is an algorithm that delivers  super resolution  images that is compatible with live cell  imaging  furthermore ricardo have collaborated  with andor  professor ricardo has collaborated with  under and we have developed surfstream  which is an algorithm in in bevit  in our fusion software  dragonfly software and in our cameras  surfstream will yell an increase in  resolution  between two to six times more in the  final data  delivering super resolution images on  the fly  in the instant while you are acquiring  the low power requirements for  surfstream make it compatible  with live cell images and can deliver  images that break the diffraction limit  as fast as 10 frames per second no  specific sample is required specific  sample preparation  is required and super resolved images  can be  delivered deep inside cells or tissues  but the dragonfly can also deliver other  imaging modalities  such as turf for example for high  resolution  cell surface events as the ones that i'm  showing here  using the thaline and the dlc1  genes in green and red respectively  but if you want to have access to more  resolution  and doing single molecule localization  microscopy you can do that  with the dragonfly using this storm  increasing the density power  of the illumination and here you have an  example of resolving  neuronal spines with this storm  dragonfly can also deliver 3d the storm  data  using an astigmatic lens  but also dragonfly can be used for deep  imaging  due to custom pinhole spacing the  dragonfly  is ideal to image deep inside cells or  tissues  as in this example of this cleared  vessel's brain  where 2.4 millimeter deep were  imaged as you can see in this video  the borealis illumination system allows  very flat illumination across the whole  field of view delivering  very high contrast and very flat  illumination which is  which is ideal for stitching and  quantification  four as for an example if you want to  image this zebrafish and  want to image it all without noticing  the tiles  due to borealis illumination you can  have the image of the whole  fish but you can acquire this fish with  the 60 hex  or the 100x lens and see the details for  example that you are interested in study  in the eye you see the details in the  aisle but you also see the whole  phenotype  of the fish stitching the whole fits  together  without any noticing of the adjacent  tiles  fusion the dragonfly software  also allows real-time deconvolution so  you can increase the signal-to-noise  ratio of your image  and also the resolution of your image  and  deal seeing what is happening in real  time with  fusion clear gpu deconvolution the  dragonfly comes in two models the  dragonfly 500  a complete solution delivering all the  imaging modalities that i had just  mentioned  and the dragonfly 200 a more compact  solution that is offered  in the upright and inverted systems  so going back to the first slide due to  very gentle illumination the dragonfly  allows you to image  avoiding phototoxicity and  photobleaching  and also delivering the temporal  resolution that you need to acquire the  dynamic events  are if either very long time lapse  or very fast dynamic experiments  the dragonfly can deliver real-time  live cell super resolution and therefore  you can acquire  the spatial resolution with all the  information you need to capture  your biological processes due  to the stitching capability the allow  the ability to acquire  multiple tiles multiple fields it will  allow you to increase productivity  of your life cell experiments  the access of multiple imaging  modalities  either confocal whitefield  storm turf surfstream allow you to  tackle  the same biological problems in a  different angle  or multiple biological problems and  therefore delivering  all you need for your imaging biological  experiments and this is  all for now i'm very happy to take any  questions  thank you all  thank you dr florindo and dr chakravarti  for your informative presentations  we will now start the live q a portion  of our webinar  now to our audience if you have a  question you would like to ask please do  so now  just click on that ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  and we will answer as many of your  questions as we have time for  okay let's get started our first  question is  dr florinda let's start with you can you  give me  a real life example that demonstrates  the productivity  of the dragonfly  uh so thank you for your question and i  imagine this comes in line with the  presentation of the spinning disk and  the productivity of using a spinning  disk  versus a point scanner so  if i think about it i remember  that in april we have posted  on twitter on our twitter account a  three color  image that had more than a 150  tile and i think more than  80 000 images and all of this  was acquired in under 30 minutes  and so i remember more or less these  members i think it's more than this  i'm not completely sure but  if it's 8 000 images more than 150 tiles  three color images it's it is very  strong  productivity of the dragonfly thank you  so much  and thank you and dr chakravarti let's  come over to you  does mitochondrial calcium uptake  regulate  drp1 oligomerization on mitochondria  that's that's a great great question in  fact i didn't have time to show the data  but  what we did show is um  the mcu knockdown which restricts the  calcium  entry into the mitochondria in that  scenario the drp1 oligomerization  on the mitochondria was absolutely fine  so which suggested that  it has uh independent pathways  and the mitochondrial calcium per se  does not determine the drp1 ability to  get onto the mitochondria  but definitely the cytosolic calcium  levels do influence and there is a  pathway for that  so thank you it's a good question  thank you and let's go ahead and have  you answer this question as well  does constriction and or imm division  require an active etc  that's a great question in fact we have  done this  so to to cut the long story short it  does influence so if you  um block the electron transport chain  using various inhibitors like the  complex one inhibitor or the complex  three inhibitor  um antimycin and bottom on uh we see a  significant decrease in the number of  constrictions  and as well as the number of divisions  so yeah you need a functional electron  transport chain  complex to be running to facilitate  those  uh constrictions that happen after  calcium  stimulation  thank you and dr florendo let's wrap  with this final question  is it possible to use photo manipulation  tools with the dragonfly  yes it is we at andor also supply photo  manipulation tools  that can use to be used with a dragonfly  or with other microscope systems  namely the micropoint and mosaic  micropoint is high power laser  for high power applications such as dna  damage  and mosaic is a device most suitable for  optogenetics  it is extremely fast and allows the  simultaneous illumination of multiple  regions  and if you want to know more about  this photo manipul